 experience flash flooding include...\nBeulah and San Isabel.\n\nThis includes the following high risk locations...\nBeulah Water Intake, Northcreek Road county line to Red Mountain Camp\nand Northcreek Road from Red Mountain Camp to Beulah.","ends":"2026-09-30T20:30:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Custer, CO; Pueblo, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.862402347fe7757438280706012f3685f2f04507.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.862402347fe7757438280706012f3685f2f04507.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued September 30 at 3:25PM MDT until October 1 at 12:00PM MDT by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Flash flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...The Southeast Mountains, the Wet Mountain Valley, Fremont\ncounty, The Pikes Peak region, and the southeast plains.\n\n* WHEN...Through Thursday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Periods of heavy rainfall associated with a remnant tropical\nstorm system will spread across southern Colorado through\nThursday.\n\n- The Southeast Mountains, including the Aspen Acres burn area, the\nWet Mountain Valley, Fremont county, the Pikes Peak region and\nthe southeast plains.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-01T12:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Northern Sangre de Cristo Mountains Between 8500 And 11000 Ft; Northern Sangre de Cristo Mountains above 11000 Ft; Southern Sangre De Cristo Mountains Between 7500 and 11000 Ft; Southern Sangre De Cristo Mountains Above 11000 Ft; Northwestern Fremont County  Above 8500Ft; Western/Central Fremont County Below 8500 Ft; Wet Mountain Valley Below 8500 Ft; Wet Mountains between 6300 and 10000Ft; Wet Mountains above 10000 Ft; Teller County/Rampart Range above 7500fT/Pike's Peak Between 7500 And 11000 Ft; Pikes Peak above 11000 Ft;